['Here's a basic outline for implementing the given task using ASP.NET Core Web API, Entity Framework Core, and Redis Cache. This solution leverages async/await, locks, and caching strategies to improve performance and scalability.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using StackExchange.Redis;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class ElementService
{
    private readonly MyDbContext _context;
    private readonly IConnectionMultiplexer _redis;

    public ElementService(MyDbContext context, IConnectionMultiplexer redis)
    {
        _context = context;
        _redis = redis;
    }

    public async Task<IActionResult> UpdateElementAsync(int pageSectionId, int elementId, string newHtmlContent)
    {
        var dbLockKey = $"db-lock:{pageSectionId}:{elementId}";
        var cacheKey = $"cache:{pageSectionId}:{elementId}";

        IDatabase redisDb = _redis.GetDatabase();

        // Try to get the cached value first
        string cachedValue = await redisDb.StringGetAsync(cacheKey);
        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(cachedValue))
            return Content(cachedValue, "text/html");

        // Acquire a lock to ensure data integrity during concurrent updates
        var lockToken = await redisDb.LockTakeAsync(dbLockKey, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
        if (lockToken.IsAcquired)
        {
            try
            {
                // Load the element from the database
                var element = await _context.Elements.FirstOrDefaultAsync(e => e.PageSectionId == pageSectionId && e.ElementId == elementId);

                // If the element is not found, create a new one
                if (element == null)
                {
                    element = new Element { PageSectionId = pageSectionId, ElementId = elementId, HtmlContent = newHtmlContent };
                    _context.Elements.Add(element);
                }
                else
                {
                    element.HtmlContent = newHtmlContent;
                }

                await _context.SaveChangesAsync();

                // Save the updated value to Redis with a timeout of 1 minute
                await redisDb.StringSetAsync(cacheKey, element.HtmlContent, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1));

                return Content(element.HtmlContent, "text/html");
            }
            finally
            {
                // Release the lock when done
                redisDb.LockRelease(dbLockKey, lockToken);
            }
        }
        else
        {
            // The lock is taken by another request, return a 409 Conflict status to indicate this
            return StatusCode(409);
        }
    }
}
```

This example includes an `ElementService` class with an asynchronous `UpdateElementAsync()` function. It uses Redis for caching and locking to handle concurrent updates gracefully. The system can scale effectively under high load, balancing between response time and resource usage.']

[' ```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.URL;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class FileLoader {
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static Optional<MoSTState> loadState(String filePath) {
        if (CACHE.containsKey(filePath)) {
            return Optional.of(CACHE.get(filePath));
        }

        try (InputStream inputStream = getInputStream(filePath);
             ObjectInputStream objectInput = new CustomObjectInputStream(inputStream)) {
            MoSTState state = (MoSTState) objectInput.readObject();
            CACHE.putIfAbsent(filePath, state);
            return Optional.of(state);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            // Log error and potential metadata retrieval issues
            // Implement external data source interaction strategy
            System.out.println("Error loading state from file: " + filePath);
        }

        return Optional.empty();
    }

    private static InputStream getInputStream(String filePath) {
        try {
            URL url = FileLoader.class.getResource(filePath);
            if (url != null) {
                return url.openStream();
            }
            return Files.newInputStream(Paths.get(filePath));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle potential file path discrepancies across operating systems
            System.out.println("Error obtaining input stream for file: " + filePath);
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}

class CustomObjectInputStream extends ObjectInputStream {
    public CustomObjectInputStream(InputStream in) throws IOException {
        super(in);
    }

    @Override
    protected Object replaceObject(Object obj) throws ClassNotFoundException, IOException {
        return (obj instanceof MoSTState) ? obj : null;
    }
}
```

This code provides a method `loadState()` that loads an object from a specified file path using an `ObjectInputStream`. It handles potential I/O exceptions and implements a custom closure mechanism with the `CustomObjectInputStream` class for efficient resource management. The method integrates with existing caching mechanisms and addresses some of the real-world engineering challenges mentioned in the prompt: compatibility across different operating systems, handling network failures gracefully, optimizing performance, and robust error handling.']

