['```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iterator>

bool updateVector(std::vector<int>& vec, int targetValue, int replacementValue) {
    auto it = std::find(vec.begin(), vec.end(), targetValue);
    if (it == vec.end()) {
        vec.push_back(replacementValue);
        return false;
    }

    vec.erase(it, std::next(it)); // erase all occurrences of the found value
    std::fill(it, vec.end(), replacementValue); // fill the erased gap with the replacement value
    return true;
}
```

This code demonstrates an optimized approach for replacing a target value within a vector in C++ while minimizing memory usage and handling edge cases such as empty vectors or vectors where all elements are equal to the `targetValue`. The time complexity is O(n), and space complexity is O(1).

The function uses the std::find algorithm to locate the first occurrence of the target value. If it is not found, a new element is appended with push_back. Then, erase() is used to remove all occurrences of the target value, and fill() fills the gap left by the erased elements with the replacement value. This approach avoids frequent resizing of the vector and ensures stable performance even for large vectors.']

